Narrator: She memorizes the molecular weights of gonadotropins. She memorizes the exact publication years of Dr. Hale's three cited papers on follicular waves. She builds a flawless, impenetrable mental fortress around that sophomore dip on her transcript, anticipating every rhetorical trap Hale might lay, every obscure textbook footnote he might weaponize to prove she hit her ceiling.
Narrator: [SPRITE: Maya_Tired, middle] By 12:15 a.m., her desk is covered in immaculate, color-coded flowcharts. She hasn't wasted a single calorie of energy on anyone else's crisis. She gets seven full hours of sleep, wakes up razor-sharp, and dresses in her borrowed navy blazer like a soldier putting on kevlar.
Narrator: [BG: Mayas_Dorm_Morning.jpg] Yet as she looks out her dorm window into the gray Friday dawn before heading to the Ag building, a quiet, sterile loneliness settles over her shoulders. She wonders, briefly, if Jordan is sitting in the 8:00 a.m. quiz room right now, hitting the exact same wall she hit two years ago, completely alone.
