        Narrator: [BG: Campus_Quad_Sunny.jpg] [BACKGROUND: The Campus Quad outside the Agricultural Sciences building, 9:55 a.m. The crisp, post-rain Friday morning sky is a brilliant, washed-clean cobalt blue. Golden sunlight glints off the wet grass and the old brick facades.]
        Narrator: [AUDIO: An uplifting, warm acoustic guitar melody, layered with the lively chatter of students and the ten o'clock bell tower chime.]
        Narrator: [SPRITE: Maya_Smiling, left] When Maya walks out of Conference Room B fifteen minutes later -- after Dr. Chen practically handed her the summer housing packet at the door and whispered that the formal offer letter would hit her inbox on Monday -- the air in the hallway feels completely different. Weightless.
        Narrator: She pushes open the heavy oak double doors of the Agricultural Sciences building, steps out onto the wide stone stairs where she stood shaking in the rain on Wednesday afternoon, and unbuttons her borrowed blazer, tilting her face up into the warm Friday sun.
        Narrator: [SPRITE: Nora_Hoodie, right] Across the wet green grass of the quad, near the center oak benches, Maya spots Nora sitting in the sunlight, lowering her cell phone from her ear with the steady, unburdened posture of someone who just set down a fifty-pound rucksack after carrying it three miles too far.
        Narrator: Two seconds later, Maya's phone buzzes in her blazer pocket. A new group chat notification pops up from Jules, titled **The Airlock Survivors**, containing a link to a meticulously color-coded Google Doc:\n*PROJECT: SATURDAY OFF-CAMPUS (REV 1.0 - NO COMPILERS ALLOWED).*
        Narrator: Grinning from ear to ear, Maya types back with one thumb as she bounds down the stone steps:\n*Maya: Finally. Please save me from bovine genetics. Also I just bodied Hale's trick question in the interview, first round of coffee at The Mill is on me!!*
        Narrator: She raises her hand and waves across the sunlit grass to Nora, who smiles and waves right back, standing up from the bench to meet her halfway.
        Narrator: Maya takes a deep breath of the crisp autumn air. She isn't ashamed of the dip in the middle of her transcript anymore, and those shaky memories have finally lost their teeth. Falling didn't stop her -- it taught her how to run. And the horizon is finally hers to chase.
